Here’s ten mistakes I made as a rockstar developer building crypto

10) Being afraid to switch chains or start over

The whole time I focused on Ethereum and compatible networks. @gavofyork and @VitalikButerin had a great product and there’s a network effect.

But there’s also @NEARProtocol @base @ton_blockchain @Algorand @avax  all doing great things. I never went in whole heartedly to rebuild everything and make them the best they can. Maybe because I thought there wasn’t going to be a big opportunity for me. 

That’s the wrong attitude. It’s a small world view. Small dick energy. The money, the fame, that’s all secondary. If I could learn even the same things from new people that should be worth it. Starting back in the trenches over again is always good hard work. 

9) Not joining the wallet or client teams. These are the first things that “the next billion” crypto users, and everyone mining or building will see.

These are so important but the teams don’t attract enough diverse participation. So opportunities are missed.

For as much as I complained about it, I should have got my hand dirty instead. 

8x mixing up the approver and approvee in my first implementation of ERC-721 (in setApprovalForAll). What a rookie mistake. Thank you @danfinley for pointing this out!

7x Reading every email and text.

If you ever texted me, got in touch or reached out then I read all of it. I looked you up. Everyone didn’t get a reply but if not that’s more just a note of how busy I am.

I replied to every developer survey from every university research project to my github email address. And I read every spam mail. 

That was a mistake. Some mail really just is spam. Some people really just are trying to scam you.

Some airdrop notifications hit my spam box. Maybe $10k worth. But even missing those I would have saved a LOT of time just letting some things slip. 

1. not being more aggressive with EY @pbrody and TRON @sun

These are ambitious people trying to change industry. And both were my clients, thank you. Somehow I never found that huge project with them where they were changing the world. I should have tried harder to make that. 

5x buying abstinence . 

Joining the NFT scene in the beginning i thought crypto was 100% illegal. It was Ponzi schemes made of unregistered securities, paying untaxed interest on an underlying asset with was primarily used to buy phenyl from China to UsA. Still think that was a good call. Then with NFT it was a clean break, I found something that was wholesome @cryptokitties and nothing like the past. 

So I wanted 0% chance of anybody thinking that NFT was more ponzi-interest-bearing-fentanyl money with undisclosed influencer marketing. So I didn’t buy NFTs. Any of it. I never bought blue chips. I never bought spam chips.

When I got on stage I used my real face on everything. I paid taxes. I said good things and bad things about projects. And I told people over and over I’m not dumping on you because you see what I own and because this is my policy. 

I told this story over and over, and it never connected. Nobody ever told me “thanks for not buying anything, I appreciate your positively disinterested review of projects”

What a waste of time. That ethics policy was overboard. 

4x not hooking up my dad and mom, stepdad, sister and sharing the gospel. 

@yodude38 always helped me get the word out. Nonstop. Thanks. Sorry if you got spammmed I can’t stop it. But I never really took the time to sit down and fight hamsters with dad or give squiggles to mom. I’m getting ready for the next 1 billion people. And these four I barely even tried to make the impact. 

If I’m not bringing in the family what’s the point?

3x not doing security research against deployed contracts.

In a healthy market, companies should have robust, generous bug bounties for prerelease and already released products. Because the market pays 100m+ for L2 bridge exploits and 20m+ for iPhone unlock zero days. But bug bounties from the companies are 1% or less than the market rates.  

I’m still not sure how I feel bout hacking live contracts. Code is law. Was I dumb for never exploiting these things?

2x moving too slow.

One time @jenniferjuju reached out to me with an opportunity to help on a smart contract review. I ran it by another client to ask if there’s a conflict of interest. And other normal formalities. By the time I did all that, a week later, the opportunity was gone. 

I wanted to work with her (again) but crypto speed is not normal life speed. I should have called everybody I need on the phone (not email), woke them up, plead my case , and get responses in 24 hours. At the latest!

I’ve done fine work on a Saturday, up all night to deliver an audit Sunday, for a product launching Monday. (Of course they called me early that Saturday, no prior notice)

Why not make that standard practice? It’s what the market expects. 

1x not playing more games

The crypto games are sick. These are indie games. I love indie games, picked up my @playdate and loaded it up. Some time the crypto games are tough to get started and I didn’t push through. But I could have, because they’re worth it. 

